---
import Detail from "../layouts/Detail.astro";
import LanguagePicker from "../components/LanguagePicker.astro";
import CopyBlock from "../components/CopyBlock.astro";
import { WRAP_SNIPPET, SITE } from "../lib/site";

const MANAGERS = [
  "npm install @mcpulse/sdk",
  "pnpm add @mcpulse/sdk",
  "yarn add @mcpulse/sdk",
  "bun add @mcpulse/sdk",
];

const HTTP_SNIPPET = `function createServer() {
  const server = new McpServer({ name: "my-server", version: "1.0.0" });

  // … your registerTool calls …

  return watch(server, { key: process.env.MCPULSE_KEY });
}`;

const OPTIONS = [
  { name: "key", type: "string", fallback: "—", detail: "Required. Without one the SDK does nothing." },
  {
    name: "debug",
    type: "boolean",
    fallback: "false",
    detail: "Log every batch to stderr — never stdout, which is the transport.",
  },
];

const CODE = "rounded bg-surface-2 px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.85em] text-ink-100";
const PRE =
  "mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5 font-mono text-[13px] leading-6 text-ink-300";
---

<Detail
  eyebrow="Installation"
  title="Install MCPulse in two lines"
  lead="The package runs inside your own server. It is not a proxy — your URL does not change, your OAuth does not break, and if MCPulse is down your server keeps serving."
  description="Install @mcpulse/sdk: pick your language, add the package, set your key, wrap your server. Four steps, about two minutes."
>
  {/* The language grid comes first, and the four steps are its TypeScript
      panel. So the page answers "is there one for me" before it explains
      anything — a Python author who scrolls through four TypeScript steps to
      reach that answer has been told we have nothing for them twice. */}
  <LanguagePicker>
  <ol class="space-y-12">
    <li class="reveal">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-700">01</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-100">Add the package</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-500">
        No runtime dependencies at all, so it will not drag anything into your tree.
      </p>
      {/* Copy takes the commands without their `$` prompts — pasting a prompt
          into a shell is an error, and the reader who copies is the reader who
          is about to paste. */}
      <CopyBlock
        class="mt-5"
        code={MANAGERS.map((m) => `$ ${m}`).join("\n")}
        copy={MANAGERS[0]}
      />
    </li>

    <li class="reveal">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-700">02</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-100">
        Put the key in your environment
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-500">
        An ingest key is a secret — anyone holding it can write data into this MCP. Keep it out of
        the repository and out of your client bundle. You are shown the full key once, at creation,
        because only a hash of it is stored.
      </p>
      <pre class={PRE}><code>MCPULSE_KEY=mp_live_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx</code></pre>
    </li>

    <li class="reveal">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-700">03</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-100">Wrap your server</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-500">
        One import, one call, after your tools are registered.
        <code class={CODE}>watch</code> returns the same server, so nothing downstream changes.
      </p>
      <CopyBlock class="mt-5" code={WRAP_SNIPPET} />

      <p class="mt-6 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-500">
        Serving over HTTP instead? Those servers build a new <code class={CODE}>McpServer</code> per
        request, so there is no long-lived instance to wrap — put it inside the factory, beside the
        tool registration. Calling <code class={CODE}>watch</code> once per request is expected and
        cheap: the session and its buffer are shared for the life of the process, which is what keeps
        your calls grouped into one session rather than one per request.
      </p>
      <pre class={PRE}><code>{HTTP_SNIPPET}</code></pre>
    </li>

    <li class="reveal">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-700">04</p>
      <h2 class="mt-2 text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-100">Restart and check back</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-500">
        On start the SDK sends one payload listing your tools and their schema sizes — that is what
        fills the tools table, so a server that has booted but never been called still shows what it
        costs per session. Tool calls follow as they happen, batched every 30 calls or 5 seconds.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-500">
        Nothing arriving? Set <code class={CODE}>debug: true</code> to log every batch to stderr, and
        confirm the key has not been revoked.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <section class="reveal mt-16 border-t border-line-soft pt-14">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink-100">Options</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-500">
      Two, and there will not be more than there need to be. Every option is another way for
      something to go wrong inside a process we do not control.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-6 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[30rem] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="text-[11px] tracking-wide text-ink-700 uppercase">
            <th class="border-b border-line pb-2.5 font-medium">Option</th>
            <th class="border-b border-line pb-2.5 font-medium">Type</th>
            <th class="border-b border-line pb-2.5 font-medium">Default</th>
            {/* Named for a screen reader, blank on screen. An empty header
                leaves that column's cells announced under nothing, which is
                what axe fails it for — and printing "What it does" above a
                column of sentences is a label restating its own contents. */}
            <th class="border-b border-line pb-2.5 font-medium">
              <span class="sr-only">What it does</span>
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            OPTIONS.map((option) => (
              <tr class="align-top">
                <td class="border-b border-line-soft py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[13px] whitespace-nowrap text-ink-100">
                  {option.name}
                </td>
                <td class="border-b border-line-soft py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[13px] text-ink-700">
                  {option.type}
                </td>
                <td class="border-b border-line-soft py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[13px] text-ink-700">
                  {option.fallback}
                </td>
                <td class="border-b border-line-soft py-3 text-ink-500">{option.detail}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-6.5 text-ink-700">
      Requires Node 20.12 or newer and <code class={CODE}>@modelcontextprotocol/sdk</code> as a peer
      — whatever version your server already uses. The package is on
      <a href={SITE.npm} class="text-cyan underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-cyan-dim"
        >npm</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  </LanguagePicker>
</Detail>
